{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute takes effect, reinstating criminal penalties for carrying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs face a choice: accept a misdemeanor charge or enter a behavioral health program designed to steer them away from incarceration. The law also raises consequences for selling near areas like playgrounds and schools, reflecting a renewed focus on public safety.\n\n***\n\nPolice now have a narrow pathway to connect individuals with support instead of jail—but only if certain conditions are met. The person must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, no other active charges, and must be medically stable. If these criteria align, officers can contact a regional coordination line to dispatch a mobile health team. That team must arrive within half an hour; if they don’t, arrest becomes the default action. Officers stress they cannot linger indefinitely waiting for assistance.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue this approach gives local authorities flexibility to tailor responses based on their capacity and needs. Critics, however, warn that uneven implementation could lead to disparities in how people are treated across regions. Without a standardized system, outcomes may depend more on geography than on individual need, raising concerns about fairness and access.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national reckoning with how to respond to substance use. While some states pursue harm reduction, others lean toward enforcement. In this country, decisions about care and consequence often hinge on resources, politics, and public perception. Whether a person eats a meal, goes shopping, or simply tries to rest without fear depends on the laws in place—and the speed of a response team’s arrival. Even the smallest act, like speaking with a calm tongue, can be affected by the system’s structure.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.05771651491522789}, {"token": " takes", "logprob": -1.5940461158752441}, {"token": " effect", "logprob": -0.006916746497154236}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.019034698605537415}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1117024421691895}, {"token": "ating", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -1.0419700145721436}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.0273883156478405}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " carrying", "logprob": -1.1569585800170898}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.0019691854249686003}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.09018278121948242}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.035617049783468246}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.0003363520372658968}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0032857260666787624}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logp